  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nZonal flow aloft through the midweek over South Central Texas means \nthe weak front is unlikely to advance much farther south into our \narea. Nonetheless, its accompanying band of higher theta-e and \nmoisture stuck beneath mid-level ridging, as well as areally lower \nconvective inhibition, will support low chances for isolated rain \n(with chances ranging up to 30 percent) through Wednesday. \nTemperatures are expected to stay unseasonably warm, the \nopportunities for slight relief from spotty rains and associated \npuffs of cloudiness allow the distribution of forecast temperatures \nfrom ensembles to gradually trend slightly cooler towards the end of \nthe upcoming week. The overall model blend is not optimistic, \nbudging only a few degrees downwards, though the statistical \nguidance is slightly more optimistic and suggests increased coverage \nof highs in the low to mid 90s by the week's end. Lows are likely to \nremain unseasonably mild, but there are hints in the ensemble \nguidance that drier continental air could penetrate into East Texas \nin the latter half of the week and help reduce low-level moisture \nenough to lower overnight temperatures.\n\nRidging remains over the area into the weekend, keeping rain chances \nlow. To the west, a strong plume of moisture is expected to kick \ninto gear as deep southerly flow intensifies on the western \nperiphery of the ridge. While the bulk of the moisture will be well \nwest of the area, a bolstered easterly flow from the Gulf running up \nthe Rio Grande in response to the jet streak aloft keeps the low \npossibility of isolated rains over the Rio Grande Plains in the \nforecast through the week. &&",
